course: books
lesson: 97
title: Just So Stories, The Butterfly that Stamped, part 2: the Butterfly stamps, Balkis is wise, and the book ends
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Balkis has heard everything. She calls the Butterfly's Wife to her finger and gives her a plan: next time he boasts, take him at his word and dare him to stamp. Soon the two butterflies are quarrelling again, and the Wife cries «Stamp! Stamp! Stamp!» The Butterfly flies back to Suleiman in a panic: now his wife will laugh at him for ever.
explain: «She will never laugh at you again,» says Suleiman, and for the Butterfly's sake, not to show off, he turns his ring. Four huge Djinns come out of the earth. The Butterfly stamps, the Djinns jerk the Palace and gardens a thousand miles into the air, thunder crashes, and all goes inky black. The Wife begs him to bring it back; he stamps again, and the Palace comes down without a bump.
explain: The quarrelling Queens rush out, terrified. Balkis tells them calmly that a Butterfly complained about his wife, and the King taught her a lesson. When they see Suleiman under his tree with a Butterfly on each hand, they fall on their faces and tiptoe back to the Palace «most mousy-quiet». The King's troubles are over.
explain: Suleiman did not even know about the Queens; he had only been playing a joke with a Butterfly. Balkis explains it was her plan, and that she was wise «First, O my Lord, because I loved you». They live happily ever after, and Kipling asks: «But wasn't it clever of Balkis?»
explain: That is the end of the twelve Just So Stories: twelve made-up «how it came to be» tales, told with the sound of a voice talking to one listening child, O Best Beloved.
fact: Balkis to the Butterfly's Wife: «next time he begins to boast, take him at his word. Ask him to stamp, and see what will happen.»
fact: The Wife: «I don't believe you one little bit ... I dare you to do it ... Stamp! Stamp! Stamp!»; the Butterfly: «I promised Suleiman-bin-Daoud that I wouldn't.»
fact: Suleiman turns the ring «just for the little Butterfly's sake, not for the sake of showing off», and four huge Djinns come; he tells them to take away the Palace when the Butterfly stamps «his left front forefoot», and bring it back «carefully» at the second stamp.
fact: The Djinns jerk the Palace and gardens «a thousand miles into the air»; there is «a most awful thunder-clap, and everything grew inky-black». In the dark the Wife cries: «Oh, I'll be good! I'm so sorry I spoke! Only bring the gardens back». They come down «without even a bump».
fact: The Butterfly says, as bravely as he can: «Of course it doesn't make any difference to me, I'm used to this kind of thing.»
fact: Suleiman: «O great wizard, what is the sense of returning to me my Palace if at the same time you slay me with mirth!» (mirth = laughter)
fact: The Queens come down the marble steps «one hundred abreast» (side by side) and say it was «a troublesome trouble, unlike any trouble we have known». An Egyptian Queen thinks Suleiman must be dead.
fact: Balkis's words to Suleiman: «First, O my Lord, because I loved you; and secondly, O my Lord, because I know what women-folk are.» The story's jokes about «what women-folk are» and «what men-folk are» belong to 1902; real people do not all fit such ideas.
fact: The end-poem says Balkis and Solomon both talk to butterflies as you would talk to a friend.
fact: Suleiman: if he had made a magic «for the sake of pride or anger», he would have been shamed, but this one was «for the sake of a jest and for the sake of a little Butterfly».
fact: The book: Just So Stories was published in 1902 by Rudyard Kipling (born in Bombay, now Mumbai, India, in 1865); he drew the pictures himself. In 1907 he became the first English-language writer to win the Nobel Prize in Literature.
fact: «Just so»: Kipling wrote that the bedtime stories for his little daughter had to be told exactly the same way each time, «just so», with not one word changed; she was the first Best Beloved.
fact: The book also holds words and ideas of 1902 about some peoples of the world that are hurtful and wrong today; we read it as an old book and say so plainly.
fact: Real life: scientists today use the words «a just-so story» for a neat-sounding explanation that nobody has tested, like how the leopard got his spots by fingertips. Real answers come from looking, testing and evidence.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «no cap» = honestly; «lowkey» = a bit, secretly; «sweet as» (New Zealand) = great, fine; «wicked» (New England) = very.
exercise: What does Balkis tell the Butterfly's Wife to do next time? | Take him at his word and dare him to stamp | Fly far away to another garden and leave him | Laugh at him | Tell the King
exercise: What does the Butterfly say when his wife dares him? | That he promised Suleiman he wouldn't | That he would stamp, but his foot hurts after flying so far today | That he is tired | That she is right
exercise: Why does the Butterfly fly back to Suleiman in a fright? | Now his wife will never believe him | He wants to borrow the ring and stamp with it himself | He is lost | He is hungry
exercise: For whose sake does Suleiman turn the ring this time? | The little Butterfly's | His own, to frighten the quarrelling Queens and show them he is the great King | Balkis's | The Djinns'
exercise: How many Djinns come out of the earth? | Four | Only one | Just two | A thousand
exercise: With which foot must the Butterfly stamp? | His left front forefoot | His right back foot, the one nearest the camphor-tree | Any foot | Both
exercise: What happens at the first stamp? | The Palace flies a thousand miles up | The Butterfly falls over on the grass and lies there waggling his wings and panting | Nothing | It rains
exercise: What does the Wife cry in the dark? | «Oh, I'll be good! I'm so sorry I spoke» | «Stamp again, stamp harder, my dear, and make it vanish for ever» | «Help, Balkis!» | «Where are you?»
exercise: What does the Butterfly say, as bravely as he can? | He's used to this kind of thing | He is very frightened | He didn't do it | He wants his mother
exercise: How do the Palace and gardens come down? | Without even a bump | With a great crash that cracks the marble steps and floods the fountain | Upside down | In pieces
exercise: «You slay me with mirth.» What does mirth mean? | Laughter | Anger | Magic | Noise
exercise: How do the Queens come down the marble steps? | One hundred abreast | One by one, very slowly and politely | Flying | In a cart
exercise: What does the Egyptian Queen think has happened? | Suleiman must be dead | A Butterfly has stamped | Balkis did a magic | There was an earthquake
exercise: What do the Queens see under the camphor-tree? | Suleiman laughing, with a Butterfly on each hand | Four Djinns standing guard over the King with swords | An empty seat | Balkis crying
exercise: What do the Queens do after seeing it? | Fall on their faces and tiptoe back | Laugh and clap their hands and run down the marble steps to stroke the Butterfly | Quarrel louder | Run away to Egypt
exercise: Did Suleiman plan to frighten the Queens? | No, he was only joking with a Butterfly | Yes, from the very first moment he saw the two butterflies quarrelling | Yes, with the Djinns | He asked the Wife to
exercise: Who planned it? | Balkis | Suleiman | The Butterfly | The Egyptian Queen
exercise: What are Balkis's two reasons for being so wise? | Because she loved him, and because she knew people | Because she had a ring and because she was the Queen of Sheba | She read books | Azrael told her
exercise: What does Kipling ask at the very end of the story? | «But wasn't it clever of Balkis?» | «Do you see?» | «Kun?» | «What shall we play?»
exercise: In the end-poem, how do Balkis and Solomon both talk to butterflies? | As you would talk to a friend | Very loudly, so that the butterflies can hear them over the fountains | In a whisper | By turning a ring
exercise: Looking back: in part 1, what did the Butterfly first boast? | That stamping would make the Palace vanish | That he was the King | That he could understand rocks and trees, like Suleiman himself | That he could fly to Sheba
exercise: Looking back: in part 1, what had made Suleiman ashamed long before? | The Animal from the sea | The quarrelling Queens, who laughed at him in front of all the animals in the world | The Butterfly | Balkis
test: Leo: «Suleiman did the magic to scare the Queens, he planned it all, no cap.» (no cap = honestly) Put it right. | He did it for the Butterfly; Balkis planned the rest | Right, he planned it all from the start | The Butterfly planned it | The Djinns planned it
test: Why does this magic NOT shame Suleiman, unlike the great dinner? | It was for a joke, not for pride | It was a much bigger magic, with four Djinns instead of one dinner, so nobody could laugh | Nobody saw it | The Djinns did it
test: What would have happened if Balkis had not spoken to the Butterfly's Wife? | The Queens might never have been frightened | Exactly the same, because Suleiman had planned to frighten the Queens that very afternoon | The Palace would have vanished for ever | Suleiman would have left
test: Which is TRUE in this part? | The Queens think the King can do anything | The Butterfly really has magic, and the Djinns obey his foot, not the King's ring | The Palace is broken | Balkis is punished
test: Suleiman calls the Butterfly «most great magician». Is he serious? | No, he is joking | Yes, the Butterfly is a real magician, and Suleiman is a little afraid of him now | He is angry | He is asleep
test: Grace from Wellington: «sweet as, so who's the real hero of this story?» (sweet as = great) What does Kipling hint? | Balkis, who quietly made the plan | The Butterfly | The Egyptian Queen | The Djinns
test: Real life: what does «a just-so story» mean to scientists today? | A neat explanation nobody has tested | A true story that has been checked again and again by careful experiments in many countries | A story about butterflies | A fairy-tale
test: Real life: where was Kipling born? | In Bombay, now Mumbai, India | In Surrey, on Merrow Down | In Brazil | In Malaysia
test: Real life: why are the stories called «Just So»? | They had to be told the same way each time | They are true, every word, just as things really happened | They are short | The animals said so
test: Looking back over the book: what did the Elephant's Child get from the Crocodile? | His trunk | His big hump | His black spots | His skin folds
test: Looking back over the book: who invented the first letter and, a week later, the alphabet with her Daddy? | Taffy | Balkis | The Woman in the Cave | The little girl in the Crab story
test: Looking back over the book: which animal said «Humph!» and got a hump? | The Camel | The great Whale | The Kangaroo | The Rhinoceros
test: Looking back over the book: whom did the Moon's Fisherman help? | The Man in the canoe | Taffy and her Daddy, by pulling the Wagai river back to the Cave | The Cat | Painted Jaguar
test: Looking back over the book: what do the Armadillo, the Crab and the Butterfly stories have in common? | A proud creature is put in its place, or a clever one wins | They all happen in a Cave | They all have Taffy | They all have Djinns
test: Max from Vermont: «lowkey every story in this book is real science, wicked.» (lowkey = a bit; wicked = very) Put it right. | They're made-up tales; science gives the true answers | Right, it is all true, and scientists use the book in their work every day | Only the Cat story is true | Only the Crab story is true
test: ★★★ The Djinns lift the Palace at the first stamp and bring it back at the second. If the Butterfly had stamped five times in all, where would the Palace be at the end? | In the air | On the ground | Half-way down | Far away in Egypt
test: ★★★ Count the twelve tales: in how many is a little girl an important character? | Three: the two Taffy stories and the Crab story | Two: only the two Taffy stories | One: the Butterfly story | Five, counting the Cat story
test: ★★★ Careful: «All the Queens except Balkis fell flat on their faces.» Who did NOT fall down? | Balkis | The Egyptian Queen | All the Queens | Nobody at all
exam: Identify the counsel given by Balkis to the Butterfly's Wife. | That upon his next boast she take him at his word and request him to stamp | That she cease to quarrel | That she depart the garden | That she address herself to the King
exam: Ascertain the number of Djinns summoned by Suleiman-bin-Daoud for the Butterfly's sake. | Four | Nine hundred and ninety-nine | Three | Only one
exam: It is given that the Queens descended the marble steps. Select the response most consistent with their subsequent conduct upon beholding the King. | They fell upon their faces and withdrew most mousy-quiet | They redoubled their quarrelling | They summoned the Djinns | They departed for Egypt
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the expression «a just-so story» as employed by scientists? | An attractive explanation lacking supporting evidence | An account established by experiment | A story concerning insects | A tale of exactly twelve parts
